recallBlock('heent-recognition','HEENT recognition','heent','AHAD seminar HEENT; integrated comparison with MUMS and supplied cases',['ahadSeminar','mums2024'],[
 ['aom','diagnosis','A toddler has acute ear pain after a URI. Otoscopy shows a bulging, opaque, poorly mobile tympanic membrane. What is most likely, and what finding separates it from isolated effusion?','Acute otitis media.|Acute symptoms plus bulging/inflammatory middle-ear findings distinguish it from asymptomatic OME.','A red eardrum alone is less specific.','Do not diagnose AOM solely from ear tugging or a crying child’s red TM.'],
 ['oe','diagnosis','A swimmer has canal swelling and marked pain when the tragus/pinna is moved. The TM does not bulge. What is most likely and what alternative must be considered if pain is severe in diabetes?','Otitis externa.|In an older/diabetic patient with disproportionate persistent pain, assess for necrotizing otitis externa.','Location and examination discriminate external canal from middle-ear disease.','Do not treat all ear pain as AOM.'],
 ['rhinitis','diagnosis','Sneezing, itchy eyes, clear rhinorrhea, pale swollen turbinates and a seasonal pattern occur without fever. What is most likely? Give THREE alternatives.','Allergic rhinitis.|Alternatives include viral URI, nonallergic rhinitis and nasal polyps/structural obstruction, depending on findings.','Itch and seasonality support allergy more than mucus colour alone.','Purulence alone does not establish bacterial sinusitis.'],
 ['gas','diagnosis','An adult has fever, exudative tonsils, tender anterior cervical nodes and no cough. What is likely, and what prevents you from calling the symptom pattern definitive?','GAS pharyngitis is plausible; use clinical assessment and appropriate testing.|EBV and other causes remain possible; the pattern/score does not prove GAS.','The MUMS scorecard guides management in specified populations, while other guidelines emphasize microbiologic confirmation.','A negative strep result does not automatically prove EBV.'],
 ['eye','diagnosis','Compare itchy watery bilateral eyes, mucopurulent lid crusting and a painful photophobic red eye. Which patterns fit allergy, bacterial conjunctivitis and an urgent alternative?','Itch/watery bilateral symptoms favour allergy.|Mucopurulent crusting favours bacterial conjunctivitis but is not definitive.|Pain/photophobia/visual change raises keratitis, uveitis or glaucoma and needs urgent evaluation.','Probability clues and red flags serve different purposes.','Do not call the third pattern ordinary conjunctivitis because there is also discharge.']
],'AHAD');
recallBlock('skin-recognition','Skin recognition','skin','AHAD seminar dermatology and existing condition comparisons',['ahadSeminar','theraSeminar'],[
 ['impetigo','diagnosis','A child develops superficial erosions with honey-coloured crusts around the nose. What is most likely? Name THREE contextual alternatives.','Nonbullous impetigo.|Consider impetiginized eczema, herpes simplex and contact dermatitis with secondary change.','Use morphology, location and history to rank the alternatives.','Grouped painful vesicles or systemic toxicity should change the assessment.'],
 ['scabies','diagnosis','Several household members itch at night; one has burrows and papules in finger webs/wrists. What is most likely, and what distribution clues matter?','Scabies.|Nocturnal itch, contact history, burrows and typical sites support it.|Infants and crusted disease may have different distribution/severity.','Contact patterns distinguish it from isolated eczema.','Post-treatment itch alone does not prove ongoing mites.'],
 ['zoster','diagnosis','Burning pain precedes grouped vesicles in a unilateral thoracic dermatome that does not cross the midline. What is most likely, and what changes urgency?','Herpes zoster.|Eye/nose/ear involvement, dissemination, neurologic symptoms or immunocompromise changes urgency.','The distribution and preceding neuralgic pain are key.','Do not use lack of fever to dismiss ophthalmic zoster.'],
 ['varicella','diagnosis','An unvaccinated child has itchy crops of vesicles and crusts at different stages, starting on the trunk. What is most likely and who needs special exposure assessment?','Varicella.|Susceptible pregnant contacts, newborns and immunocompromised contacts need prompt assessment.','Lesions at mixed stages and a generalized distribution differ from dermatomal shingles.','A high-risk contact’s prophylaxis is a separate task from the child’s symptom care.'],
 ['ulcers','diagnosis','Compare a shallow draining gaiter-area ulcer with edema to a painful distal punched-out ulcer with cool skin and weak pulses. What diagnoses fit and what treatment must wait for perfusion assessment?','Venous ulcer versus arterial ischemic ulcer.|Assess arterial supply before compression; severe ischemia/rest pain requires urgent vascular assessment.','Appearance alone is not enough to decide compression strength.','Do not prescribe strong compression for every leg ulcer.'],
 ['melanoma','diagnosis','A pigmented lesion is evolving and differs from the patient’s other nevi. What features raise concern and what confirms the diagnosis?','Asymmetry, irregular border, colour variation and especially evolution/ugly-duckling change raise concern.|Appropriate full-thickness biopsy/histopathology confirms the diagnosis; choose technique/referral appropriate to the lesion/site.','A diameter under 6 mm does not exclude melanoma.','Do not reassure solely because one ABCDE feature is absent.'],
 ['nec','followup','A skin infection has rapidly increasing pain out of proportion to visible changes, systemic illness and new bullae. What is your disposition?','Emergency evaluation for possible necrotizing infection and urgent surgical assessment.|Do not delay transfer while arranging a routine outpatient test or oral antibiotic trial.','Time and source control are critical.','A modest-looking early rash can coexist with a serious deep infection.']
],'AHAD');
recallBlock('cardio1','Module 5 · Cardiology I','cardio','AHAD seminar cardiology begins at source block 1239; 2025 Hypertension Canada comparison',['ahadSeminar','htn2025'],[
 ['scope','reasoning','Which cardiology topics must remain in your AHAD Modules 1–5 midterm review?','Include the first cardiology section: hypertension, lipid/CV-risk assessment, PAD/venous disease, heart failure, cardiac examination and murmurs identified in your seminar notes.|Do not label all cardiology as final-exam-only.','You explicitly confirmed Module 5 begins cardiology; later cardiology content is not automatically included.','Seminar emphasis is not a guarantee of exact item weighting.'],
 ['htn','diagnosis','Repeated standardized clinic BP is 136/84 mmHg. Under the 2025 Canadian primary-care guideline, is this below the diagnostic threshold? What confirms the assessment?','No. Hypertension is defined at BP ≥130/80 mmHg under the 2025 guideline.|Confirm using standardized technique and out-of-office readings when appropriate; assess white-coat/masked patterns.','Diagnosis and medication-start thresholds are different questions.','A single rushed clinic reading does not establish the whole management plan.'],
 ['measurement','reasoning','List THREE corrections before interpreting an apparently high clinic BP.','Use a validated device with an appropriate cuff.|Seat/rest the patient with supported back/arm and feet flat, and avoid talking.|Repeat standardized measurements and review recent caffeine/exercise/smoking and home/ambulatory values.','Measurement quality changes classification.','Do not treat a technical artifact as resistant hypertension.'],
 ['crisis','followup','BP is markedly elevated with acute dyspnea and new neurologic symptoms. Is the decision based only on whether diastolic BP exceeds 130?','No. Suspected acute target-organ injury defines an emergency needing immediate assessment.|Evaluate neurologic, cardiac, renal and retinal concerns and arrange emergency care.','The seminar’s single diastolic cutoff is not a safe stand-alone rule.','Severe BP with symptoms must not wait for an arbitrary number.'],
 ['hf','diagnosis','A patient has exertional dyspnea, orthopnea, edema, raised JVP and basal crackles. What diagnosis is likely, which test assesses structure/function, and what other causes matter?','Heart failure is likely; echocardiography assesses cardiac structure and function.|Consider renal disease, pulmonary disease and other causes of dyspnea/edema; acute hypoxia/distress needs urgent care.','Combine history, examination and objective testing.','Edema alone does not prove heart failure.'],
 ['jaw','reasoning','A 54-year-old has exertional fatigue and jaw discomfort on stairs. Name THREE cardiac possibilities and the finding that would change this to an emergency.','Consider angina/ischemia, acute coronary syndrome and heart failure depending on the full picture.|Current/prolonged or rest symptoms, instability, dyspnea or other acute features need emergency ACS assessment.','Assess ischemic equivalents in people of any sex.','Do not dismiss ischemia because the patient does not describe central chest pain.'],
 ['pad','diagnosis','Calf discomfort reliably starts with walking and resolves with rest. ABI is 0.78. What is likely? How would ABI 1.5 change your interpretation?','Peripheral arterial disease with claudication is likely.|ABI above about 1.4 may reflect noncompressible calcified vessels; consider toe-pressure/TBI or vascular testing rather than calling it normal.','Diabetes and renal disease can make an ABI misleading.','Rest pain, tissue loss or an acutely cold pulseless limb needs urgent assessment.'],
 ['jvp','reasoning','You cannot identify a venous waveform during JVP examination. Can you document “no JVD”? What should you do and write?','Optimize position/light/neck alignment and distinguish venous from arterial pulsation.|If still not visualized, document “JVP not visualized/assessable,” not a confident normal result.','Absence of a visible waveform is not evidence of a normal venous pressure.','JVP is conventionally recorded as a height; do not copy the seminar’s ambiguous “3 mmHg” note as a measured result.'],
 ['murmur','reasoning','A seminar line describes systolic murmurs as the opening of the mitral/tricuspid valves. What is wrong, and how do you classify a murmur?','Systole lies between S1 and S2; mitral/tricuspid closure contributes to S1, and these valves are generally closed during ventricular systole.|Describe timing, site, intensity, radiation and response to maneuvers, and assess symptoms.|A new diastolic murmur is generally pathologic and needs evaluation.','This corrects a source-note error while preserving the original for comparison.','Do not confuse the sound marking a valve event with the direction of abnormal flow.'],
 ['lipids','reasoning','Before interpreting dyslipidemia in Module 5, what risk information belongs beside the lipid panel? Give at least THREE.','Age, smoking status and blood pressure/treatment.|Family history of premature cardiovascular disease and possible familial dyslipidemia.|Diabetes, CKD and established ASCVD; decide whether a risk score is appropriate or risk is already high.','A lipid number alone does not determine the whole cardiovascular risk assessment.','Do not infer low risk just because HDL is high.']
],'AHAD');
recallBlock('foundations','Assessment and prescribing foundations','foundations','AHAD/Thera seminar foundations; official exam scope takes precedence over early provisional notes',['ahadSeminar','theraSeminar'],[
 ['sensitivity','reasoning','A highly sensitive test is negative in a patient whose pretest probability is very high. Can you say disease is impossible?','No. A negative result lowers probability by an amount related to the test’s likelihood ratio and performance in that population.|Reconcile a discordant result with the history/examination and consider repeat/alternative testing when indicated.','SNOUT is a useful heuristic, not a guarantee independent of context.','Do not let a mnemonic erase a high-risk presentation.'],
 ['specificity','reasoning','A low-prevalence condition has a positive screening test. What must you consider before calling it confirmed disease?','Consider pretest probability, specificity/false positives and confirmatory testing.|Explain that screening and diagnostic confirmation serve different purposes.','Predictive values vary with prevalence.','A positive test is not automatically the final diagnosis.'],
 ['nonpharm','reasoning','An exam asks for THREE non-pharmacologic interventions. A student lists acetaminophen, an herbal product and saline gargles. How many clearly meet the requested category?','Only the saline gargles clearly meet the requested category in this course context.|OTC medicines and medicinal herbal products are still pharmacologic.|Give three distinct behavioural, physical, environmental or supportive-care actions.','Read the requested category before generating the answer.','“No prescription required” does not mean non-pharmacologic.'],
 ['definitions','reasoning','Distinguish pharmacokinetics, pharmacodynamics and bioavailability using a medication example.','Pharmacokinetics: what the body does to the drug—absorption, distribution, metabolism and elimination.|Pharmacodynamics: drug effects/mechanism and concentration–effect relationship.|Bioavailability: fraction of an administered dose reaching systemic circulation unchanged.','Explain the concept rather than only expanding an abbreviation.','A low systemic bioavailability does not mean every topical drug is safe for every patient.'],
 ['inhibition','reasoning','A drug inhibits metabolism of a concurrently administered active substrate. What might happen to exposure, and why is the rule not universal for prodrugs?','The active substrate may accumulate, increasing adverse effects.|For a prodrug requiring that pathway for activation, inhibition can reduce active-metabolite formation and efficacy.|Check the actual drug/pathway and clinical relevance.','This makes the seminar inhibitor/inducer mnemonic more clinically useful.','Do not assume every CYP interaction has the same direction for every medicine.'],
 ['prescription','calculation','Whether the exam supplies the drug or expects you to choose it, what medication information should your prescription make unambiguous?','Drug name, strength and formulation.|Exact dose, route, frequency, indication when PRN and definite duration when applicable.|Quantity consistent with the directions and explicit repeats; include required patient/date/prescriber identifiers in a full script.','You can practise choosing a regimen separately from transcribing a supplied regimen.','Never invent real identifiers; use the school’s provided practice identifiers.']
],'AHAD');
